<header class="about-hero">
  <div class="shell about-hero-grid">
    <div><p class="page-kicker">About Adrian</p><h1>I had to ask myself a harder question.</h1><p class="page-lede">Not “How do I grow this agency?” But “Is this still the business I want to build?”</p><p class="page-lede">That question took me from Upstack Studio to Second Team. Here’s the story.</p></div>
    <figure><img src="/assets/images/adrian/reading.webp" alt="Adrian Ching reading" width="1009" height="1800" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Still learning. Still building.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="section">
  <div class="shell about-narrative">
    <p class="section-index">The story so far</p>
    <div class="story-chapter"><span>2017</span><div><h2>I started by building for other people.</h2><p>I founded Upstack Studio in 2017. For years, my team and I built web and mobile software with business owners.</p><p>The job went beyond getting an app to work. There were customers to understand, choices about what belonged in the product and a business that needed the work to be worth it.</p><p>That’s where I learned to look past the feature list. The hard part was rarely just the code.</p></div></div>
    <figure class="wide-story-photo"><img src="/assets/images/adrian/upstack-team.webp" alt="Adrian Ching with the Upstack Studio team" loading="lazy" width="1130" height="1130"><figcaption>The Upstack Studio team.</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="story-chapter"><span>The work</span><div><h2>A working product is only part of the answer.</h2><p>Take Teleme. The app connected people with doctors through video calls and messaging. Our team worked from prototyping through launch.</p><p>The point wasn’t the software itself. It was helping a person speak with a doctor from home.</p><p>I carry that question into every new idea: what becomes better for the person using this?</p></div></div>
    <div class="story-chapter"><span>The decision</span><div><h2>I shut the agency down.</h2><p>I could have kept improving the model. But it was no longer the company I wanted to build. I wanted to own the products, own the results and focus on fewer, bigger ideas.</p><p>If you’ve built a business, you may know how hard it is to separate “I can keep doing this” from “I should keep doing this.”</p><p>For me, the answer was to stop running the agency and build something else. The experience came with me. The business model didn’t.</p></div></div>
    <div class="story-chapter"><span>A new chapter</span><div><h2>AI changed how I think about the next company.</h2><p>Software is getting faster and cheaper to make. That opens up more possibilities. It doesn’t tell us which ones deserve our time.</p><p>I’m interested in what a small team can do when AI handles repeatable work and people keep judgment, relationships and the important decisions.</p><p>That’s the direction I’m building in now.</p></div></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-dark" id="what-im-building">
  <div class="shell about-build-grid">
    <div><p class="section-index">What I’m building</p><h2>One main company.<br>One growing portfolio.</h2></div>
    <div class="build-ambition"><span>01</span><h3>Second Team</h3><p>Second Team is my main company. It gets most of my time and it is the company I’m building for the next decade.</p><p>I keep the product work private and share the lessons as I build.</p></div>
    <div class="build-ambition"><span>02</span><h3>My portfolio</h3><p>Alongside Second Team, I’m building a portfolio of software products, media and online businesses that I own and operate.</p><p>This website is where I share the work, including the parts that do not go to plan.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="shell values-grid">
    <div><p class="section-index">How I work</p><h2>A few rules I come back to.</h2></div>
    <div class="values-list"><p><span>01</span>Start with the business problem, not the tool.</p><p><span>02</span>Tell the truth, even when the answer is “do not build it.”</p><p><span>03</span>Do fewer things and give them proper attention.</p><p><span>04</span>Let AI handle repeatable work. Keep people on judgement and relationships.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="photo-break"><img src="/assets/images/adrian/conversation.webp" alt="Adrian Ching in a recorded founder conversation" loading="lazy" width="1013" height="1800"><div><p class="section-index">A few founder conversations</p><h2>You don’t have to make every decision alone.</h2><p>Maybe the team is busy, the follow-up is uneven and you’re being asked to approve one more tool. You can see the problems. What’s harder is knowing which one to act on.</p><p>I still help a small number of founders think those choices through. It’s a focused part of my work alongside Second Team.</p><a class="button" href="/work-with-me/">See how I can help</a></div></section>

